<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mir Jafar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1691–<a href="/years/1765">1765</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mir Jafar was the commander of the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraj-ud-daulah">Siraj ud-Daulah</a>, Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, who secretly allied with the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> and held back his troops at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plassey">Battle of Plassey</a> in 1757. Installed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Robert Clive</a> as nawab, he depended on the Company, which replaced him in 1760 with his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-qasim">Mir Qasim</a> and restored him in 1763. His name became a byword for treachery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mir-jafar.jpg" alt="Mir Jafar" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mir_Jafar_(left)_and_Mir_Miran_(right).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mir Jafar was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> around 1691. His grandfather Syed Husayn Tabatabaei migrated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najaf">Najaf</a> and settled in Delhi in 1675 at the invitation of the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>, married the emperor's niece and served as a qadi at court. Mir Jafar's paternal aunt, Begum Sharfunnisa, was the wife of Nawab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alivardi-khan">Alivardi Khan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. When Raghoji I Bhonsle raided Bengal with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> forces from 1747, Mir Jafar, then subedar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, withdrew all his forces until Alivardi Khan arrived and routed Raghoji at the Battle of Burdwan. The angry nawab dismissed him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alivardi-khan">Alivardi Khan</a>'s successor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraj-ud-daulah">Siraj ud-Daulah</a>, Mir Jafar served as principal general and led the army to victory over the British on 19 June 1756. Siraj nonetheless favoured his rival Raja Manikchand, and the discontented Mir Jafar joined other opponents of the nawab, including the banking family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagat-seth">Jagat Seths</a>. They approached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, whose agent William Watts sent the Armenian Khwaja Petrus Aratoon to investigate. Mir Jafar, as paymaster of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> army, offered 2.5 crore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a> for help in removing the nawab. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Robert Clive</a>, who thought Siraj universally hated, joined the plot.</p><p>Mir Jafar betrayed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraj-ud-daulah">Siraj ud-Daulah</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plassey">Battle of Plassey</a> in 1757, and the British installed him as a dependent nawab after Siraj's defeat and execution. He paid 17,700,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a> in compensation to the Company and the traders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, and Clive received over two million rupees in bribes. Finding the Company's demands boundless, he turned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, whom the British beat at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinsurah">Chinsurah</a> in November 1759. The Company then forced him to abdicate in favour of his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-qasim">Mir Qasim</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-qasim">Mir Qasim</a> proved able and independent, and came into dispute with the Company over its refusal to pay taxes to him. The Company overthrew him and restored Mir Jafar, and Mir Qasim went to war with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula">Shuja-ud-Daula</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> and the Mughal emperor Shah Alam II, who were defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-buxar">Battle of Buxar</a> on 22 October 1764. Mir Jafar died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murshidabad">Murshidabad</a> on 5 February 1765, after leprosy, prolonged ill health and an addiction to opium.</p><p>Many historians see his reign as the start of British expansion over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>. The Company abolished the nizamat in 1793 and took complete control of the province. He is reviled as a traitor, especially among Bengalis, and the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-iqbal">Muhammad Iqbal</a> condemned Mir Jafar and Mir Sadiq as a stigma on humanity, on religion and on the country.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mir Jafar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Clive's victory at the Battle of Plassey in 1757 deposed the Nawab of Bengal Siraj-ud-Daulah in favour of <b>Mir Jafar</b>, and the Battle of Buxar in 1764 forced the emperor Shah Alam II to appoint the Company diwan, or revenue collector, of Bengal, Bihar and Orissa in 1765.</blockquote><a href="/empire/company-rule-in-india">Company rule in India · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Helped by the defection of <b>Mir Jafar</b>, Clive's East India Company troops defeated Siraj ud-Daulah.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-23">23 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Robert Clive's victory at Plassey in 1757, helped by <b>Mir Jafar's</b> betrayal, made the nawabs dependent on the East India Company.</blockquote><a href="/state/nawabate-of-bengal">Nawabate of Bengal</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comilla">Cumilla</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>. Died in Bengal, in what is now <a href="/countries/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=23.45,91.2&amp;year=1739" aria-label="Cumilla on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6983 1299 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mir Jafar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7433 1569h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7433 1569h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=23.45,91.2&amp;year=1739">What was here in 1739? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
